# Dependency-wave implementation

Stage 3 implements all approved stories for a requirement. When a
requirement produced more than one story (multi-repo), don't implement
them in an arbitrary order or all-at-once — group into **waves** by
dependency, then run each wave's stories **in true parallel** via separate
subagent `Task` calls (per the locked decision in `pipeline/DESIGN.md`
§10.3), one task per story in that wave.

## Computing waves

1. For each approved story, identify what it actually needs to exist
   first — a real dependency, not just "same requirement":
   - A frontend story that calls a new backend endpoint depends on that
     endpoint's story.
   - A story that relies on a new graphify node/edge type depends on that
     `graphify` change landing (and its rebuild) first.
   - Two backend stories touching unrelated tables/routes have no
     dependency on each other, even in the same requirement.
2. Topologically sort: Wave 1 = stories with no dependencies on other
   stories in this requirement. Wave 2 = stories depending only on Wave 1.
   Continue until every story is placed.
3. State the computed waves explicitly before starting implementation
   (e.g. in each affected repo's `.pipeline/<slug>/state.json` or as a
   message to the user) —
   this itself isn't a separate gate, but should be visible, since it
   determines execution order and what "in progress" means at any moment.

## Executing a wave

- Dispatch one `Task` subagent per story in the wave, in the same
  turn/batch — not sequential Task calls waited on one at a time.
- Each subagent works on its own branch
  (`pipeline/<requirement-slug>/<repo>`) and only touches its own repo —
  waves are safe to parallelize because stories are scoped to different
  repos (or at minimum, non-overlapping files); if two stories in the
  same wave would touch the same file, that's a real dependency you
  missed in step 1 — split them into different waves instead of hoping
  the parallel edits don't collide.
- A wave is "complete" once every subagent in it has finished its
  implementation (Stage 3 only — critique/traceability/QA still follow
  per-story, not per-wave). Don't start Wave N+1 until Wave N's code
  changes are actually in place, since Wave N+1 may need to read them.

## When there's only one story

Skip the wave computation — a single-story requirement is trivially
"Wave 1 of 1", no parallelism needed.
